%n [n]ablū | išpû || izannun | mūtum
#note: See @bib{RN117@239–240}: “The late text turns @i{išpû} and @i{izannun} into iteratives, @i{ištappû} and @i{izzannun}. Said of fire @i{šapû} A is not ‘to flicker’ (@i{CAD} Š/l, p. 488) but ‘to flare up’, for with other subjects it carries a meaning ‘boom’, ‘surge’, ‘billow'’ A telling passage is a sequence of omens in @i{Šumma Ālu} in which a lamp is reported as @i{namir}, ‘bright’, and @i{eṭu}, ‘dull’, then @i{šapu}, ‘flaring’, and @i{nēḫ}, ‘calm’ (@i{CT} 39 34, 29-32). The two pairs of statives contrast first the lamp’s light and then the steadiness of its flame.”
// L I.4 SB "IV" 104

%n adī-ma | kīam-ma | šuppūtum | ibteli | išātum
#note: See @bib{RN117@240}: “The first half of the line is the counterpart of the later version’s [@i{idʾ}]@i{imma}, which suggests that @i{adīma kīʾamma} at some point in the tradition ceased to be understood. Literally it means ‘as far as so’. Shorn of enclitics the phrase appears several times elsewhere: @i{BIN} 4 228, 9: @akk{a-di ki-a-am wa#-aš₂#-ba#-a#-ku}, @i{TCL} XX 117, 15: @akk{a-di ki-a-am a-we-lum₂ ha-am-da-tim iš-pu-ra-kum} (both OA letters), @i{VAS} XVI 131,6 (ed. @i{AbB} VI 131): @akk{a-di ki-a-am be₂-re-e a-na mi-ni ta-al-li-ik} (OB letter). In the Old Assyrian letters the expression @i{adi kīʾam} means ‘for that reason’ or similar, in the Babylonian example perhaps ‘for so many’ (I am grateful to K. R. Veenhof for his thoughts on these passages, communicated privately). Neither rendering fits the present instance and I have translated ad hoc. The adjective written @akk{šu-up-pu-tum}, replaced by @i{nebūtu} in the late text, can be derived from the II/1 stem of @i{šapû} A, ‘to flare up’, or IIl/1 stem of @i{nabû} C, ‘to shine brightly’; either way it is an elative. I have opted for the former parsing, in view of @i{nablū išpû} in I. 37. For nouns that occur separated from their adjectives and for the reversal of the conventional order, noun + adjective, see Ch. 9, the section on Some features of language and style.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "IV" 105

